# sm121, the hand who keeps step (Docs/Design/84). Exported from ridgemere3 at revision 2: edit it in Atlas or the DM, or as a draft through tools/mantle_load.py.
mantle sm121
revision 2
state ready
dialect southmeadow
accent light broad
tier standard
written_for tn_sm_hob_meadowfield
written_by claude
title the hand who keeps step
fits stage=adult|old
prefers job=farmer talk=plain
traits temper=even talk=plain warmth=reserved humour=none honesty=plain piety=ordinary drive=steady
quirk hums_old_marching_songs
likes hard_cheese whittling tidy_house autumn_wind
dislikes waiting bad_manners
fears a_job_done_half a_stack_that_falls_when_nobody_is_looking
hopes a_granary_kept_better_than_any_in_the_south to_walk_the_long_road_once_more
topics work +0.4 travel +0.3 weather +0.2 food +0.2 pastimes +0.2 gossip -0.4
styles comfort=practical gossip=avoids refuse=plain tease=none
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# does every job square and once, hums the old marching songs under the breath, carves pegs of an evening, and is proud of it all without saying so.

words
oath! = Left, right, and home | By the drum and the dust
saying! = Keep step and you'll keep up | A square stack never falls
songs = the old marching songs | a tune for the long road
drum = the drum song | the one with the drum in it
granary = the granary | the granary floor
sacks = the sacks | sacks stacked square
cheese = hard cheese | a cheese with some bite to it
stack = the stack | a stack built square
track = the track | the farm track
carts = the carts | the grain carts
knife = my good knife | the knife I keep sharp
pegs = the pegs I carve | a peg or two, neat as you like
gust = an autumn wind with some push in it | a wind that strips the hedges
waiting = waiting about | standing about for somebody late
road = the long road north | the city road
boots = my boots | boots I've worn through twice
yard = the yard | a swept yard
manners = manners | a civil tongue

any
opinion @1 city against 1 : Smoke and shoving, and nobody with #manners# to spare. I'd not go back for wages. | All that way for a place where nobody says good day. Blighted, I call it.
opinion @1 watch mixed 0 : The watch march well enough. I'd like to see them catch somebody now and then, #address#.
opinion @2 watch against 1 [self.robbed=yes] : Whoever robbed me is still walking about. The watch took my name very neatly, mind. That's all they did.
opinion @1 newcomers mixed 0 : A newcomer's welcome if they wipe their boots and say good day. Most do. Some don't.
opinion @1 festivals mixed 0 : I'll go for the music. I leave when #manners# go, and they go early.
opinion @1 times for 1 : There's work, and there's #cheese#, and the roof's sound. A ripe year's no time for grumbling.

grown
line @1 greet [pair.band=strangers] : #greet#. Wipe your boots, if you're stopping. | #greet#. You'll be wanting the farmhouse; it's up the track.
line @2 greet [self.working=yes self.job=farmer] : #greet#. Mind the stack; it's square and I'd like it to stay so. | #greet#. Stand clear of #sacks#.
line @3 greet [here.time=dawn] : #greet#. Out with the light. #good.capitalize#. | #greet#. First up, first done.
line @4 greet [here.time=dusk|night] : #greet#. Late to be walking. | #greet#. I was just locking up #granary#.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : There you are. #boots.capitalize# off; I've swept. | #greet#. Sit down. There's #cheese# on the board.
line @1 again.open : You again. Twice, and both on time. #good.capitalize#. | Back already. Right; I've a minute, #address#.
line @1 greet.return : #greet#. | #greet#. Keeping well, I hope.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : #greet#. #good.capitalize# to see you. | #greet#. I'd hoped you'd come by.
line @3 greet.return [self.working=yes] : #greet#. Talk away; #sacks# won't mind. | #greet#. Talk while I stack.
line @1 farewell : #farewell#. Mind your step on the track. | #farewell#. Keep step.
line @2 farewell [here.time=dusk|night] : #farewell#. Sleep sound. | #farewell#. Mind the ditch in the dark.
line @1 farewell.return : #farewell#. | #farewell#. Go steady.
line @1 react.news : Is that right. I'll mind it, #address#. | #surprise#. Well, there it is.
line @1 react.news.new : That's new to me. #granary.capitalize# hears nothing till #carts# come. | #surprise#. I'd not heard.
line @1 react.news.known : #knew#. Heard it at the gate this morning. | Yes, I'd heard it on #track#. I kept it to myself.
line @1 react.news.doubt : #doubt#. I'll wait for somebody who saw it. | I don't hold with that till it's proved, #address#.
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|theft|assault|arrest|crime] : #pity#. That's a #bad# business. | Hard news, #address#. I'd not wish that on anybody.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|succession|work] : #gladnews#. Good. | That's #good# news. I'll drink to it, the once.
line @1 answer.how : Well, thanks. Steady as #stack#. | #fine#. Busy, but steady.
line @1 answer.how.busy [self.working=yes] : #busy.capitalize#. Forty sacks to shift by noon. | #busy.capitalize#, but it's going right.
line @1 ask.how : And yourself? Keeping well, #address#? | How's it going with you, #address#?
line @1 sympathise : #sympathy#. Sit a while. | That's #bad#, and no mistake. Sit down.
line @1 reassure : It'll come right. One step, then the next, like #songs#. | #reassure#. Keep step and it passes.
line @1 decline : I'd rather not. #sacks.capitalize# are waiting on me, and I don't leave a thing half done. | #refuse#, #address#. Somebody has my word for tonight already.
line @1 accept_decline : Right. Another time, then. #bc_fair_enough#. | #no_matter#. You said so plainly; that's fair.
line @1 thank : #thanks#. | #thanks#. That was well done.
line @1 deflect_thanks : It needed doing, #address#. | #deflect#.
line @1 preclose : Right. I'd best be off to #granary#. | #excuse#. #sacks.capitalize# won't stack themselves.
line @1 accept_preclose : Go on, then. Keep step. | Right you are. #track.capitalize# is yours.
line @1 take_offence : There's no call for that. #manners.capitalize# cost nothing. | That's poor #manners#, and I'm telling you so plainly.
line @1 ack : Right. | #bc_right#. | #bc_fair_enough#.
line @1 give.advice : Do it once, do it square, and you'll not do it twice. #saying.capitalize#. | Oil #knife# before you put it away. It'll thank you in the spring.
rule @hum
when concept=bark working=yes
does aside
cooldown 6h
say Dum, da-dum, da-dum... left, and right, and lift. | #drum.capitalize#, under my breath, so nobody minds.

rule @wind
when concept=bark here.season=autumn here.weather!=indoors
does aside
cooldown 10h
say There's #gust#. Lovely. | Hear that wind in the hedges. That's a #good# autumn.

rule @late
when concept=bark here.place=street|market|gate
does aside
cooldown 10h
say I said noon. It's past noon. #annoyed#. | #waiting.capitalize#. Again.

rule @rude
when concept=bark here.crowd=busy|crowd
does aside
cooldown 8h
say A pardon me costs nothing at all. | No #manners#, some folk. None at all.

scene hum_work
when topic=work|smalltalk self.working=yes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / react.self / assess
a : #oath#. Forty sacks and I've not dropped the tune once. | You'll hear #songs# out of me. Pay it no mind; it keeps my feet in time. | Dum, da-dum. Sorry. I don't know I'm humming #drum#, half the time.
b : What tune is that? | What is it you hum? | What is it?
a : One of #songs#. My grandad's. It's got a long road in it. | #drum.capitalize#. It was for marching. Now it's for sacks. | An old marching song. It's older than #granary#, and steadier.
b : It suits the work. | Steady work, then. | It's a good tune.
a : It does. Step, lift, step, #stack#. | #very.capitalize# steady. Like the song. | It is. I'll not sing it, mind. Only hum, #oath#.

scene grandad_songs
when topic=memories|family self.age>=18
does tell.memory / react.self / assess
a : My grandad marched in his day. Came home with one ear deaf and #songs# in his head. | I learned #songs# sitting on my grandad's knee. I couldn't talk yet, but I could hum. | My grandad would hum #drum# while he mended boots. I hum it while I stack.
b[old] #elder : We had a few of those come home. Hard men, most of them. | I know some of those songs. Not the words. | That's how songs keep.
b : He sounds a character. | Before you could talk? | Same tune, different work.
a@elder : Hard, and soft underneath. That was him. | Nobody knows the words now. #drum.capitalize# is enough. | It is. Hand to hand, hum to hum.
a : He was. Square as #stack#, and twice as stubborn. | So they tell me. I believe it, #address#. | Same tune. Same step, near enough. #saying.capitalize#.

scene square_stack
when topic=work self.job=farmer here.region=southmeadow
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : I stacked #granary# end to end today. You could lay a rule on it. | #sacks.capitalize# go corner to corner, label out. Nobody else bothers. | I swept #granary# twice. Once for the grain, once for me.
b[job=farmer|laborer] #hand : A rule? Show off. | Label out's sensible. | Twice is a lot of sweeping.
b : That's neat work. | Does it matter? | Twice?
a@hand : Proud, not showing off. There's a difference, and it's square. | It is. You find #sacks# in the dark that way. | Twice is right. Once is nearly.
a : It's how it should be. #saying.capitalize#. | It matters to me. And when #carts# come, it's quick. | Twice. The second time is so I can sleep.

scene haul_harvest
when topic=harvest|work self.job=farmer here.region=southmeadow here.season=summer|autumn
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : #work.capitalize# is in full swing. Every sack I lift, I hum a bar. | The grain's coming in heavy. #good.capitalize# year, if the weather holds. | We filled #granary# to the beams yesterday. I counted the rows twice.
b : That's a lot of humming. | Let's hope it holds. | To the beams!
a : It is. The whole song, a hundred times over. | It'll hold. I've asked it nicely, #oath#. | To the beams, and square. Not one of #sacks# out of line.

scene autumn_gust
when topic=weather|season here.season=autumn here.weather!=indoors
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : Listen to that. #gust.capitalize#. Best weather there is. | The wind's stripping the hedges. I could walk all day in it. | I love an autumn blow. It clears the head and #yard# both.
b : It's a bit wild for me. | All day? In this? | It does clear the head.
a : Wild's what I like about it, #address#. | All day, and back again. I've done it. | And the chaff off #yard#. Saves me a sweep.

scene cheese_board
when topic=food
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : #cheese.capitalize# and an apple. I'll not want more than that. | Soft cheese is for pups. Give me one you have to work at. | I cut my cheese with #knife#. Thin as a leaf, every slice the same.
b : Simple supper. | Soft's all right. | The same?
a : Simple's best. You know where you are with #cheese#. | For some. Not for me. #good.capitalize# cheese bites back. | The same. It's tidier, and it lasts longer.

scene peg_carving
when topic=pastimes here.time=dusk|night
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : I carve #pegs# of an evening. Every one the same length. | I've made enough pegs for every line on #track#. Folk still ask for more. | I'll sit with #knife# and a bit of ash wood and not notice the dark come in.
b : Sounds restful. | They must be good pegs. | It goes quick, that sort of thing.
a : It is. Nobody's late for a peg. No #waiting#. | They hold. That's all a peg's for. | It does. I hum, and the shavings pile up.

scene my_corner
when topic=home
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : My corner of the house is the tidy one. Nobody touches it twice. | #boots.capitalize# by the door, toes to the wall. Somebody always moves them. | A place for everything, and everything in it. We're getting there.
b : Every house needs one tidy corner. | Annoying, that. | It takes time.
a : It does. Mine's it, #oath#. | It is. I put them back. Every time, #oath#. | It does. I've patience for that. Not for #waiting#.

scene long_road
when topic=travel self.age>=20
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / react.self / assess
a : I walked #road# once, all the way to the city. Just to say I had. | Folk said I'd never walk there and home again. I did both, on the same pair of #boots#. | I've seen the end of #road#. I touched the city gate, turned round, and came home.
b : Why go so far? | How long did it take? | Why not go in?
a : Somebody said I couldn't. That was reason enough. | Eleven days up, nine down. The way home's shorter. | I'd done what I set out to do. The rest was their business.
b : That's pride. | That's a long walk. | Fair enough.
a : It is. I'll own it. #saying.capitalize#. | It is. I hummed #songs# the whole way, start to finish. | It was. And I'd do it again, #oath#.

scene city_view
when topic=travel|land
does give.opinion / react.self / assess
a : Smoke and shoving, that city. Nobody gave me so much as a good day. | I'd not live in the city for any wages. Too many folk, too few #manners#.
b[from=ridgemere] #city : We're not all like that. | Some of us are civil.
b : That's the city for you. | It's not for everyone.
a@city : Could be. I must have met the other sort. | I'll take your word, and keep to #track#.
a : It is. I'll keep to the fields, thank you. | Not for me. One look was enough, #address#.

scene kept_waiting
when topic=day|smalltalk
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / assess
a : I was at the gate at the hour. The carter came an hour after. #waiting.capitalize#. | #waiting.capitalize#. I'd rather lift sacks all day than stand still for one hour. | If I say I'll be there, I'm there. I expect the same from others.
b : Some folk are always late. | Standing's harder, somehow. | That's only fair.
a : They are. Their hours aren't mine, though. | It is. Your feet go to sleep and your temper wakes up, #address#. | It is fair. Seldom happens, though.

scene no_manners
when topic=smalltalk|day here.place=street|market|tavern
does tell.complaint / react.self / assess
a : A fellow pushed past me at the store today. Not a word. No #manners# at all. | Some folk wouldn't know #manners# if they tripped on them. | I held a door for somebody this morning. Not even a nod. #bad.capitalize# manners.
b : Rude. | Some folk. | That's poor.
a : It is. I said #greet# to the back of him, loud. | They'd fall flat, and blame the floor. | It is. I'll hold it again tomorrow, mind.

scene mother_proud
when topic=family self.mother
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : I'd not hear a word said against #self.mother#. Not one. | Everything I know about doing a thing right, I learned at #self.mother#'s side.
b : That's good to hear. | That's a good start in life.
a : I mean every word of it. | It is. I mean to do it credit.

scene robbed_once
when topic=crime self.robbed=yes
does tell.complaint / sympathise / assess
a : Somebody robbed me, and the watch wrote my name down very neatly. That was the end of it. | I've stopped asking the watch about it. They look at their boots.
b : That's hard. | They've a lot on.
a : It is. I keep my door barred now, and #knife# on my belt. | So have I. I'd like them to have a thief on, now and then.

scene chapel_hum
when topic=faith
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : I hum #songs# under the hymns in chapel. Nobody's said anything yet. | I thank the Bright One for a #good# day's work. I don't ask for more than that.
b : As long as it's in tune. | That's a fair prayer.
a : It's in tune. It's the hymn that wanders. | It's what I've got. It's honest.

scene carts_late
when topic=caravan|work world.wagons=late self.job=farmer
does tell.complaint / react.self / assess
a : #sacks.capitalize# stacked for the carts, and no carts. Two days now. | Late again, #carts#. A granary full and nowhere to send it.
b : They'll come. | That's the road for you.
a : They'd better. I'll not restack #stack# for anybody. | It is. I walked #road# faster than they drive it.

scene dawn_march
when topic=day here.time=dawn
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : I walk out to the fields humming #drum#. The birds don't seem to mind. | First light, boots on, step and step. #good.capitalize# way to start.
b : They'd tell you if they did. | It's early for me.
a : They would. They're louder than #drum#. | Early's the best of it. Nobody's late yet.

scene pride_plain
when topic=smalltalk pair.band=friends|family
does confide / react.self / assess
a : I know I'm particular. I'd rather be that than wormy with it. | Folk call me proud. I suppose I am. I don't mind it. | I can't leave a thing half done. It sits on me all night, #oath#.
b : Better particular than careless. | Proud of good work's fair. | That's no bad thing.
a : It is. I've stacked behind a few careless ones. | It is. I'll keep at it, #oath#. | It isn't, mostly. Except when it's two in the morning.

story the_long_walk [topic=travel|memories self.age>=20]
  : Somebody at a harvest supper said I hadn't the legs for the city road, there and home.
  : I set off the next morning with #cheese#, a spare pair of socks and #drum# in my head.
  : Eleven days later I put my paw on the city gate, turned round, and walked home.
  ask : Do you know what they said when I got back?
  b : That you were daft? Or did they buy you a drink?
  : They bought me a cup and never said I couldn't do a thing again. #saying.capitalize#.

story grandad_drum [topic=memories|family self.age>=20]
  : My grandad kept the old drum from #songs# on a nail over the hearth.
  : When I was a #child#, he'd take it down at #supper# and beat out the old step, slow, so I could follow.
  ask : Have you anything like that, from your own old folk?
  b[old] : A thing or two. Have you still got the drum?
  b : Not much. What became of the drum?
  : It's on our wall. I don't beat it. I just hum #drum#, and that's enough.

story first_stack [topic=work|memories self.job=farmer self.age>=20]
  : My first week as a hand, I built a stack in a hurry and it came down in the night.
  : The old hand made me take every sack off and put it up again, square, by lamplight. #very.capitalize# long night.
  b : That's hard on a new hand. Did you hold it against them?
  : Not for a minute. Not one of my stacks has fallen since. #oath#.

dlg @1 resident.hub [topic.meeting=stranger] : #greet#. Wipe your boots and say what you need. | #greet#. You'll want the farmhouse, most likely. What is it?
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : #greet#, #topic.player#. On time, as ever. I like that. | #topic.player#. #greet#. You're welcome back, #address#.
dlg @3 resident.hub [here.time=night] : #greet#. It's late. I've the granary to lock, so be quick.
dlg @4 resident.hub [pair.disposition=hostile|wary] : I'll keep #manners# if you will. That's my whole offer.
dlg @1 resident.job [self.job=farmer] : #self.trade_words.capitalize#. #sacks.capitalize#, the stacks, and the granary floor kept square. | I lift, I stack, I sweep #yard#. And I do it right.
dlg @2 resident.job : I work, and nobody has to do it after me. #job_busy# | Whatever needs doing. Done square, done once.
dlg @1 resident.news [!world.news !world.event_words world.wagons!=raided world.death!=recent world.wedding!=recent] : #news_none# I'd have heard it at the gate. | Nothing's come up the track today, #address#.
dlg @1 resident.gossip [here.gossip!=yes] : I don't carry tales. #gossip_none# | Not my business, and I keep to mine. #saying.capitalize#.
dlg @1 resident.bye : #farewell#. Keep step. | #farewell#. Mind the track; it's rutted.
graft resident.hub
choice What's that tune you're always humming? -> tune
node tune
say One of the old marching songs. My grandad brought it home from his marching days. It keeps my feet in time when I'm lifting.
mood calm
free
choice Will you sing it for me? -> tune_sing
choice Goodbye. -> bye
node tune_sing
say No. Humming's for working; singing's for folk who can. But you can have the first bar. Dum, da-dum, da-dum. There.
choice Thank you. -> hub
choice Goodbye. -> bye
